  "surface": {
    "_comment": "Road-surface LINE layer (Dated/2026-08-09-surface-line-tiles-design.md, owner-approved 2026-08-10). Zero DB rows: ways stream straight to GeoJSONL and tippecanoe. The seven class names are the client's SURFACE_STYLE keys and are pinned to it by a cross-language test.",
    "minZoom": 8,
    "maxZoom": 13,
    "highways": [
      "cycleway",
      "track",
      "unclassified",
      "residential",
      "tertiary",
      "secondary",
      "primary",
      "living_street",
      "path"
    ],
    "gatedHighways": {
      "path": {
        "key": "bicycle",
        "values": [
          "yes",
          "designated"
        ]
      }
    },
    "classes": {
      "paved": [
        "asphalt",
        "concrete",
        "concrete:plates",
        "concrete:lanes",
        "paved",
        "paving_stones",
        "chipseal"
      ],
      "gravel": [
        "gravel",
        "fine_gravel",
        "compacted",
        "pebblestone"
      ],
      "pave": [
        "sett",
        "cobblestone",
        "unhewn_cobblestone"
      ],
      "dirt": [
        "dirt",
        "earth",
        "ground",
        "mud",
        "sand",
        "grass",
        "woodchips"
      ],
      "rock": [
        "rock",
        "stone"
      ]
    },
    "untaggedClass": "unverified",
    "todo": {
      "_comment": "The \"needs a tag\" arm is NOT every untagged way — it is the ways whose answer is genuinely unknown. Measured on Belgium's tagged ways (2026-08-12): a primary road is unpaved 0.1% of the time, secondary 0.5%, tertiary 2.5%, cycleway 0.0%, residential 7.2% — and since mappers tag the surprising road first, an UNTAGGED one of those classes is safer still. Asking a rider to go and record them is asking them to confirm asphalt. Tracks (88% unpaved), paths (50/50) and rural unclassified lanes (6.8%) are where nobody can predict the answer, and they are a third of the bytes: 43.3 MB -> 16 MB for Belgium. | 2026-08-12: tertiary and cycleway were briefly added here and then withdrawn. The gap they were meant to fix (an untagged tertiary on a signed route, e.g. the Zuiderdijk) is real, but highway class is the wrong key for it: the rule that survives is ROUTE-AWARENESS — an untagged way carrying a signed route or node network is homework whatever its class, because a rider rides it BECAUSE it is signed. Implement with the routes extractor (docs/plans/handoffs/2026-08-12-routes-layer-and-surface-quality.md).",
      "highways": [
        "track",
        "path",
        "unclassified"
      ],
      "minZoom": 11,
      "maxZoom": 13
    },
    "gaps": {
      "_comment": "The planning half of the same question. Per-cell km of unrecorded to-do network, so \"where should I go scanning?\" is answered by ~900 squares per country (0.5 MB for Belgium) instead of 400,000 line geometries. cellZoom 12 is ~6 km at 50N. maxZoom hands over to the to-do lines exactly where they begin (todo.minZoom), so one legend row means squares at planning zoom and roads at riding zoom.",
      "cellZoom": 12,
      "minZoom": 4,
      "maxZoom": 11,
      "tileProps": [
        "km",
        "pct",
        "n"
      ]
    },
    "tileProps": [
      "cls",
      "hw",
      "ref"
    ],
    "_roadTypeChannel": "Road type is NOT a colour in this scale (owner 2026-08-12). `highway=cycleway` used to override the surface class, which answered two questions with one key. The raw highway value rides every feature as `hw` (tileProps) and the client draws a cycleway as a pale core inside whatever surface colour it has."
  }
